A: <p>Let's start with background information...</p>

<p>A <code>JFrame</code> is a container for a <code>JRootPane</code>, which contains the <code>contentPane</code>, <code>JMenuBar</code> and <code>JGlassPane</code></p>

<p><a href="https://i.sstatic.net/suHcp.gif" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/suHcp.gif" alt="RootPane"></a></p>

<p>When you override <code>paint</code> of top level container like <code>JFrame</code>, you are only painting the bottom most component, the <code>JRootPane</code> and it's contents are then painted over the top, making it kind of pointless.</p>

<p>See <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/javase/tutorial/uiswing/components/rootpane.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">How to Use Root Panes</a> for more details</p>

<p>Painting is also a complex operation, failing to call <code>super.paint</code> will cause no end of issues, make sure you always call the super paint method before painting unless you really understand how it works and are prepared to do it's job manually.</p>

<p>In Swing you are instead encouraged to extend from a <code>JComponent</code> based class (<code>JPanel</code> been the preferred) and override its <code>paintComponent</code> method and perform your custom paint there</p>

<p>This component can either be added to the window or set as the <code>contentPane</code> or added to some other container depending on your needs.</p>

